[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 2"""
 date="2026-10-09T16:36:22Z"
 content="""
A way to fix this while keeping autoenable could be to make git-annex
refuse to push annexed files to remotes by default after autoenabling a
special remote. 

The UI for this could look something like:

	> git-annex sync
	[...]
	(autoenabling special remote "foo")
	[...]
	get somefile (from foo) ok
	copy somefile (to origin)
	git-annex: Error, sending files to all remotes is disabled because
	the recently autoenabled special remote "foo" has not been verified
	to be safe. Please run "git-annex verifyremote foo" to do that.
	push origin ok

	> git-annex verifyremote foo
	This is an automatically enabled special remote, with
	this configuration:
		type=S3 bucket=foo
	Is this remote repository one you expected to be using?
	If so, run:  git-annex verifyremote foo --safe
	If not, run: git-annex verifyremote foo --unsafe

What should verifyremote --unsafe do? At that point files can
have already been gotten from it. Deleting the whole local repository
might be a good move. It would need to advise the user about what to do.

This fix is incomplete in that it still lets the attacker
learn if I do have a given key in a location that they hypothesized.
`git-annex sync` will get it from the autoenabled special remote,
and update location tracking to say it's in my clone of the repository,
and pushes the git-annex branch to origin, exposing that information
to the attacker.
"""]]
